From caf6f4a9771e71dfb1fb3bc10c1051ef13918f19 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 10:00:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] state: compose the corruption-scope authorizer,
 widen scope to blob/sequence reads, skip checks once latched

Review + full-suite findings on the scoped quick_check confirmation:

- The scope recorder replaced the connection's authorizer, silently dropping a
  caller's deny rule (test_heal_rebuild_rolls_back_atomically). SessionDB
  connections now open as ScopedAuthorizerConnection, whose set_authorizer
  records the caller's callback so the recorder is composed in front of it
  (caller verdict returned unchanged) and removed without clearing it. A
  connection without that slot is never touched: scope unknown, whole-file check.
- The writer-connection replay blocker (in_transaction + PRAGMA shrink_memory
  under the writer lock) moves to hermes_state_corruption_confirm as
  writer_replay_blocker: it is writer-connection state, not a data read, so it
  keeps the lock; the Pattern-C gate is unchanged.
- Scoped checks always include sqlite_sequence (AUTOINCREMENT, no authorizer
  callback) and every shadow table of a virtual table in scope (FTS5 reads
  <name>_data via the blob API). Real damaged-page regressions for both.
  Documented: a scoped quick_check does not check the freelist or page
  ownership; a recurrence still latches.
- An already-latched file never spawns a check (reads and writes); spawn-count
  regression.
- The child loads exactly the tokenizer the parent connection already loaded
  (core fts5_cjk_so_path), so a whole-file check of a contentful
  cjk_unicode61 FTS5 table no longer fails "SQL logic error"; without it the
  check errors and latches (fail closed, documented and tested).
- notice_guarantee.candidates entry: one-shot corrupt + clean confirm -> no
  latch; recurrence -> latch after one check.
- Docs: concurrent writers / cross-process bounds of the snapshot check, and
  that a later clean quick_check does not prove the file was never damaged.
